* *Shosha Kongenki* (circa 1530-1540), *Miyasegawa*
* *Shoshinki* (1543), *Miyasegawa*
* *Jingi Shiryō*, Shibunkaku – Kurita Hiroshi, *Nakatahama Shrine*, *Miyasegawa*
* *Kofudoki Itsubun Kōshō*, Arimine Shoten – Kurita Hiroshi, *Nakatahama Shrine*, *Miyasegawa*
* *Ancient Historical Geography Studies*, Sanyōdō / Bitchū Province / Miyasegawa, Sanseidō – Inoue Michiyasu, *Miyasegawa*
* *Kibi District History*, Meicho Publishing – Nagayama Usaburō, *Nakatahama Shrine*
* *Detailed Records of 22 Shrines* (1874, Meiji 7), Okayama Prefecture Shrine and Temple Bureau, *Nakatahama Shrine*, *Miyasegawa*
* *Bitchū Kayo District Shrine Records* (February 1889, Meiji 22), Kayo District Office, *Nakatahama Shrine*
* *Great Japanese Place Name Dictionary* (1900, Meiji 33), Fushobo – Yoshida Tōgo, *Miyasegawa*
* *Kibi District Shrine Journal* (Kogō Shrine section), Okayama Prefecture Shinto Association Kibi Branch – Takasugi Tsuchizō, *Nakatahama Shrine*, *Miyasegawa*
* *Monthly Historical Journal / The Birth of the Ancient Kibi Clan* (1976, Volume 4, Issue 6), Meicho Publishing – Kazuhara Katsuto, *Miyasegawa*
* *Okayama Prefecture Archaeological Report No. 35 / Bitchū Kōmori Kofun*, Okayama Prefecture Board of Education – Kazuhara Katsuto, *Miyasegawa*
* *Kibitsuhiko Legends and Tsukiyama Kofun* (*Kibi, But Still Kibi*), *Ancient Kibi Province Study Group* – Demiya Tokuhisa, *Miyasegawa*
