'''Shinmei Shrine''' (神明神社) is a [[:en:Shinto shrine|Shinto shrine]] located in [[:en:Sōja, Okayama|Sōja City]], [[:en:Okayama Prefecture|Okayama Prefecture]], Japan.

== Overview==
The shrine was historically the tutelary deity of [[Abe Village (Okayama)|Abe Village]] (阿部村) in [[Kayo District, Bitchū Province|Kayo District, Bitchū Province]].<ref>Abe Village was located in what is now southern Fukui, Sōja City. In [[:en:1881|1881]] (Meiji 14), it merged with Fukui Village.</ref> The enshrined deity is [[Amaterasu]], and the shrine is associated with the [[Ise Grand Shrine]], specifically the [[Kōtai Jingū]] (Inner Shrine). 

The shrine’s sacred messenger is the [[:en:Chicken|Chicken]] (long-crowing bird), which played a role in luring Amaterasu out of the [[:en:Ama-no-Iwato|Ama-no-Iwato]] (Heavenly Rock Cave).

An excerpt from the lost [[Fudoki]] of Bitchū Province, titled *Miyasegawa* (宮瀬川), states that in Kayo District, there was a river to the east of the *Ise Mikami no Yashiro* (伊勢御神社), and to the west of the river stood the *Kibi Takehiko no Mikoto no Miya* (吉備建日子命宮). This river was thus called *Miyasegawa*. Some theories equate *Ise Mikami no Yashiro* with Shinmei Shrine, though this remains uncertain. Additionally, records indicate that the shrine was known as *Motoise Nakatahama Shrine* in several historical texts.

Today, the shrine operates independently.

== Sub-Shrines==
* Kōjin Shrine (荒神社)
* Ji Shrine (地神社)
* Kin Shrine (金神神社)
* Kimoto Shrine & Shōden Shrine (木本宮・正殿宮)
* Nanasha Shrine (七社宮)

== Historical Records==
* *Engishiki Jinmyōchō Tōchū* (1503), Yoshida Library – Urabe (Yoshida) Kanekazu, *Miyasegawa*
* *Shosha Kongenki* (circa 1530-1540), *Miyasegawa*
* *Shoshinki* (1543), *Miyasegawa*
* *Jingi Shiryō*, Shibunkaku – Kurita Hiroshi, *Nakatahama Shrine*, *Miyasegawa*
* *Kofudoki Itsubun Kōshō*, Arimine Shoten – Kurita Hiroshi, *Nakatahama Shrine*, *Miyasegawa*
* *Ancient Historical Geography Studies*, Sanyōdō / Bitchū Province / Miyasegawa, Sanseidō – Inoue Michiyasu, *Miyasegawa*
* *Kibi District History*, Meicho Publishing – Nagayama Usaburō, *Nakatahama Shrine*
* *Detailed Records of 22 Shrines* (1874, Meiji 7), Okayama Prefecture Shrine and Temple Bureau, *Nakatahama Shrine*, *Miyasegawa*
* *Bitchū Kayo District Shrine Records* (February 1889, Meiji 22), Kayo District Office, *Nakatahama Shrine*
* *Great Japanese Place Name Dictionary* (1900, Meiji 33), Fushobo – Yoshida Tōgo, *Miyasegawa*
* *Kibi District Shrine Journal* (Kogō Shrine section), Okayama Prefecture Shinto Association Kibi Branch – Takasugi Tsuchizō, *Nakatahama Shrine*, *Miyasegawa*
* *Monthly Historical Journal / The Birth of the Ancient Kibi Clan* (1976, Volume 4, Issue 6), Meicho Publishing – Kazuhara Katsuto, *Miyasegawa*
* *Okayama Prefecture Archaeological Report No. 35 / Bitchū Kōmori Kofun*, Okayama Prefecture Board of Education – Kazuhara Katsuto, *Miyasegawa*
* *Kibitsuhiko Legends and Tsukiyama Kofun* (*Kibi, But Still Kibi*), *Ancient Kibi Province Study Group* – Demiya Tokuhisa, *Miyasegawa*
* *Kibitsuhiko Legends (Part II)*, Kibi Local Culture Research No. 19, Shujitsu University Kibi Local Culture Research Institute – Demiya Tokuhisa
* *Sōja City History* – General History Volume, Ancient and Medieval Historical Documents, Archaeological Records (Bitchū Fudoki section), Sōja City, *Miyasegawa*
* *Kibi Corridor* (Published in *Sanyō Shimbun*, April 14, 1996), Sanyō Shimbun, *Miyasegawa*

== Access==
* **Rail:** Approximately 10 minutes on foot north from [[JR West Japan|JR West]] [[:en:Momotarō Line|Momotarō Line]] ([[:en:Kibi Line|Kibi Line]]) at [[:en:Higashi-Sōja Station|Higashi-Sōja Station]].

